read in while loop

# Inside a line-by-line while loop, the read command functions as an input engine that pulls exactly one line of text at a time, stores it in a variable, and tells the loop whether to keep going or stop.

1. The Core Action (read)
By default, the read command waits for data from an input stream (which is fed into the loop by the file at the bottom or other ways). It scans the incoming text from left to right until it hits a newline character (\n).

Once it finds the end of the line, it stops reading, slices that segment off the stream,and stores it in some variable and moves the file pointer down to the next line, waiting for the next iteration of the loop.


2. The Backslash Safeguard (-r)
The -r option stands for raw input.
• Without -r: Bash treats backslashes (\) as escape characters. If your file contains a path like C:\Users\Name, Bash will drop the backslash and output C:UsersName. If a line ends in a backslash, Bash thinks the line is continuing onto the next row.
• With -r: Bash treats backslashes as literal characters. C:\Users\Name stays exactly as it is written.

basically it will stop escape characters from functioning and get it in raw data

3. The Target Storage (line)
This is simply a variable name chosen by you. You could name it row, data, or x. The read command takes the text it just swallowed and stores it inside this variable. You can then access it inside the loop using $line.

[File Stream] ──> Line 1 ──> read command sees text ──> Success (0) ──> Loop Runs
[File Stream] ──> Line 2 ──> read command sees text ──> Success (0) ──> Loop Runs
[File Stream] ──> End of File (EOF) ──> read finds nothing ──> Failure (Non-Zero) ──> Loop Breaks

read will return exit status
As long as read successfully captures text, (Exit status 0). 
When the file runs out of text (End of File, or EOF), read tries to grab a line but finds nothing. (Exit status 1).

so you can use this read command inside while loop as condition

while read -r line; do
    echo "Processing: $line"
done < data.txt

cat data.txt | while read -r line; do
    echo "Processing: $line"
done

# Lists all .log files and processes them one by one
ls *.log | while read -r logfile; do
    echo "Found log file: $logfile"
done


