IFS stands for Internal Field Separator. It is a special environment variable that determines how Bash recognizes word boundaries and splits strings into individual pieces.

Think of IFS as Bash's default settings for delimiter characters. Whenever Bash needs to parse a sentence, read a line, or expand an array, it consults IFS to know exactly where one word ends and the next begins.


 The Default Value of IFS

By default, IFS contains three specific whitespace characters:
1. A space ( )
2. A tab (\t)
3. A newline (\n)
Because of this default behavior, if you type for word in Hello World Structure, Bash splits on the spaces and processes three distinct words.
so by default your text have spaces or tab space or new line then bash seperates them into individual values, not as single value when it is passed as list or collection

How IFS Works Inside a while read Loop

When reading files line-by-line, IFS drastically changes how the read command handles your data.

Scenario 1: Clearing IFS to Preserve Whitespace (IFS=)

while IFS= read -r line; do
    echo "$line"
done < file.txt

Setting IFS= ensures the line is read exactly as it appears in the file, preserving all leading and trailing whitespace.
For this specific read operation, there are absolutely no word delimiters.

Scenario 2: Changing IFS to Parse Structured Files (CSV / Columns)
If you intentionally want to slice a line into distinct variables based on a specific character, you can redefine IFS.
# Imagine a file entry: "john:admin:/bin/bash" (Standard Linux /etc/passwd format)

while IFS=':' read -r user role shell; do
    echo "User $user uses the $shell environment."
done < users.txt

How it works: By setting IFS=':', you instruct the read command to stop splitting on spaces and tabs, and only split the text when it hits a colon

why IFS most of the time written before read

You will often see IFS altered in two different ways in scripts. One is safe, and one can break your script.

❌ The Unsafe Way (Global Change)
IFS=':'

If you change IFS globally on its own line, it alters how everything in the script behaves from that point forward. Later on, standard loops or word expansions might break because Bash is no longer recognizing spaces as word separators.


The Safe Way (Local/Inline Change)

IFS=':' read -r var1 var2

When you prefix a command with a variable assignment like this, Bash applies that change only for the execution of that single command. The moment the read command finishes a line, IFS instantly snaps back to its normal, default whitespace settings.

