break and continue in loops

In Linux Bash scripting, break and continue are loop control statements used to alter the standard flow of execution inside loops (while, until, and for).

Without them, a loop simply repeats blindly until its core condition is met. These two keywords allow you to react dynamically to changes or specific data inside the loop.


1. The break Statement (Exit Completely)

The break statement instantly terminates the entire loop and jumps straight to the code following the done keyword. It completely kills the loop, regardless of whether the loop's original condition is still true.

Best Used For:

• Escaping an infinite loop (while true).
• Stopping early once you've found what you were looking for (saving CPU cycles).
• Aborting the script if a critical error or file corruption is detected.


Practical Example:

Imagine you are scanning a file for an administrative error. The moment you find it, you want to stop processing and exit.

#!/bin/bash

while read -r log_line; do
    if [[ "$log_line" == *"CRITICAL_FAILURE"* ]]; then
        echo "🚨 Critical error detected! Stopping immediate processing."
        break # Exits the while loop entirely
    fi
    echo "Processing line: $log_line"
done < system.log

echo "Script has moved past the loop."

2. The continue Statement (Skip and Move On)

The continue statement skips the remaining commands inside the current iteration of the loop and instantly jumps back up to the top to start the next iteration. It does not kill the loop; it just bypasses the current step.


Best Used For:

• Skipping blank lines or commented lines (#) when reading a configuration file.
• Filtering out data you don't care about before executing complex commands.
• Bypassing errors on a single file so your script can move on to the next file without crashing.


Practical Example:

Imagine you have a text file of user accounts, but some rows are blank or are comment lines starting with #. You want to skip those cleanly.

#!/bin/bash

while read -r username; do
    # Skip empty lines or comment lines
    if [[ -z "$username" || "$username" == #* ]]; then
        continue # Instantly jumps back to the top to read the NEXT line
    fi

    # This code only runs if the 'continue' was NOT triggered
    echo "Creating system folder for user: /home/$username"
done < users.txt

Advanced Tip: Breaking out of Nested Loops (break N)

By default, break and continue only apply to the innermost loop you are currently running. However, if you are running a loop inside another loop, you can pass a number N to control outer layers:
• break 2 terminates the current loop and the outer loop surrounding it.
• continue 2 skips the rest of the current inner loop and triggers the next iteration of the outer loop.

for folder in /data/*; do
    while read -r file; do
        if [[ "$file" == "STOP_ALL" ]]; then
            break 2 # Breaks out of BOTH the while loop and the outer for loop
        fi
    done < "$folder/manifest.txt"
done
